          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "zipp/glob.py's Translator.translate_core (used by Path.glob/Path.rglob) compiles a caller-supplied glob pattern into a regex that is then run against every entry in the archive's name list, with no length or complexity cap on the incoming pattern and no timeout/backtracking guard around the resulting regex match -- restrict_rglob only rejects a '**' that is not alone in a path segment, it does not bound overall pattern size. Path.glob, Path.rglob and Translator.translate are all entry-point-reachable (hop_distance=0), so a caller that forwards an attacker-influenced glob pattern into Path.glob gets no resource-exhaustion protection from the library.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read zipp/glob.py directly: Translator.translate_core (lines 44-57) calls self.restrict_rglob(pattern) then builds a regex via separate()/replace() with NO length or complexity bound on `pattern` anywhere in the call chain. restrict_rglob (lines 70-82) only raises if '**' appears in a segment that isn't the whole segment (`if any('**' in segment and segment != '**' for segment in segments): raise ValueError(...)`) -- it does nothing to bound overall pattern size or wildcard count. The replace() method (lines 59-68) turns every literal '*' into `[^{seps}]*` and every '**' into '.*', so a pattern with many alternating literal-chars and '*' in one segment (e.g. 'a*a*a*a*...*a') compiles into a regex with many back-to-back unbounded wildcard groups separated by literals (e.g. `a[^/]*a[^/]*a[^/]*...a`) -- the textbook catastrophic-backtracking shape for a backtracking engine like Python's `re`, with no outer bound on how large the attacker can make it. Confirmed the actual call site: zipp/__init__.py Path.glob (line 401-409) takes `pattern` directly from the caller, does only an empty-string check, then does `re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern)).fullmatch` against every `data.filename` in `self.root.filelist` -- no timeout, no re.DOTALL-safe limiter, no length cap before compiling/matching. Path.rglob (line 411-412) just forwards to glob() with `f'**/{pattern}'`. Queried the graph (scan_id=8dc6047e6a7ea4f1758850043b9d62e68ffe0915): Path.glob, Path.rglob, Translator.translate, and Translator.translate_core are all reachable_from_entry=true with hop_distance=0 (zipp/__init__.py:401, zipp/__init__.py:411, zipp/glob.py:27, zipp/glob.py:44), matching the analyst's reachability claim exactly. This repo is the zipp library itself (only test files call .glob()/.rglob()), so there is no concrete in-repo attacker-to-sink path demonstrated -- the finding is a library-level weakness: any downstream caller that forwards an untrusted glob pattern into Path.glob/rglob inherits this unbounded-regex-construction/match risk. exploit_search returned 'corpus unavailable: not indexed', so I could not cross-check a published Metasploit module/CVE for this exact zipp version (no (:Dependency) node for zipp exists in the graph either, so I could not pin an exact version) -- this is noted as an unresolved advisory gap, not used to reject."
          },
