        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Path.glob(pattern) is a public API entry point whose 'pattern' argument is attacker-controlled (EntryPoint, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0). It is passed to Translator.translate(pattern), which builds a DOTALL regex literal via self.extend: rf'(?s:{pattern})\\Z'. Inside translate_core/replace, every literal '**' segment in the caller-supplied glob pattern is converted via re.escape(...).replace('\\\\*\\\\*', r'.*') into an unanchored '.*' fragment, and consecutive '**' segments (a syntactically valid glob, e.g. '**/**/**/**/x') are joined only by the literal path separator, producing a composed pattern such as (?s:.*/.*/.*/.*/x)\\Z. Because the wrapper is DOTALL, each '.*' can consume separators too, so the fragments fully overlap -- a classic multi-wildcard catastrophic-backtracking shape functionally equivalent to nested quantifiers like (.*)* . This compiled pattern is then applied with .fullmatch against every filename pulled from the (potentially attacker-supplied) zip archive's filelist at zipp/__init__.py:407-409, so both the glob pattern and/or the zip entry names can be attacker-influenced. A crafted multi-'**' pattern matched against a crafted/non-matching long filename can drive Python's backtracking re engine into superlinear/exponential time, causing a denial of service. The repo's own tests/test_complexity.py (test_baseline_regex_complexity, test_glob_depth, test_glob_width) explicitly assert constant/linear-time behavior for this exact glob-matching path, confirming the maintainers treat this code as a regex-complexity risk area.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source + graph, not from the analyst's narrative. (1) Source at zipp/__init__.py:401-409 shows Path.glob(self, pattern) builds `re.compile(re.escape(self.at) + tr.translate(pattern)).fullmatch` and applies it to every `data.filename` in `self.root.filelist` (zip entry names) \u2014 pattern is a direct, unsanitized parameter of a public method. (2) zipp/glob.py:27-42 (Translator.translate/extend) confirms the regex is wrapped `rf'(?s:{pattern})\\Z'`. (3) zipp/glob.py:59-68 (Translator.replace) confirms every literal '**' is turned into an unanchored `.*` via `re.escape(match.group(0)).replace('\\\\*\\\\*', r'.*')...`, joined only by the literal separator from the original pattern text. (4) zipp/glob.py:70-82 (restrict_rglob) only enforces that '**' occupies a whole segment \u2014 it never bounds how many '**' segments a pattern may contain, so a caller-supplied pattern like '**/**/**/**/x' is accepted and compiles to a chain of several un-anchored `.*` tokens separated only by '/'. Because '.' already matches '/' in Python's re (independent of the (?s:) DOTALL flag, which only affects '\\n'), these sequential wildcard tokens overlap ambiguously when matched against a filename containing many '/'-like characters, which is the standard \"multiple-ambiguous-wildcard\" ReDoS shape (polynomial blowup whose degree grows with the number of '**' segments the attacker chooses to supply) \u2014 a real instance of CWE-1333, not merely nested-quantifier mimicry. (5) Graph confirms reachability independent of the analyst: `MATCH (m:CpgMethod) WHERE file_path='zipp/__init__.py' AND name IN ['glob','rglob','match']` returns all three with reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, and matching `EntryPoint` nodes (exposure='reachable', kind='handler') exist for Path.glob/Path.rglob; the re.compile/fullmatch/translate/translate_core call sites at __init__.py:407 and glob.py:27/31/44 are all reachable_from_entry=true at hop_distance=1 from those entry points. (6) tests/test_complexity.py (test_baseline_regex_complexity, test_glob_depth, test_glob_width, lines 70-97) does assert constant/linear big-O for this exact glob-match path, though only for a simple `*.txt` pattern \u2014 it does not itself exercise the multi-'**' case, so it corroborates complexity-risk awareness but is not direct proof of the specific multi-wildcard blowup (I verified the regex-construction mechanism analytically rather than by executing it, since no code-execution tool was available in this session). Exploit-reference check: mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"not indexed\" (corpus unavailable) for 'zipp glob pattern regular expression denial of service', so I could not cross-check against a published module/CVE version range; I did not find a NEWS.rst entry describing a fix for this exact multi-'**' issue in this snapshot (top entry is v3.19.0), so patched-status is unconfirmed either way \u2014 treat exploitability/patch-state as unverified by that channel, independent of the source-level confirmation above."
          },
